 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Landing 6 of docs/plan.md#the-order (deps, data), approved by root 2026-11-15; it replaces this entry's 2026-11-11 text and seon.merge/one-fact-model-for-the-merge, whose four rules it deletes. EXTENDS seon.merge (the one decision) and the writer's merge ops in root's writer, reading Datahike's d/merge-base and d/changes (deps' seon.merge/changed-datoms); inside Seon, no script. The rule: (merge into from base), the same for main and any branch: from's net change since base, as facts [e a v] each with the source tx that made it, is applied whole to into, each source tx replayed as a new later tx through the checked write (seon.db/transact-checked) with its metadata and :seon.tx/source, in one commit with both parents. A conflict is only an [e a] (cardinality one) or [e a v] (many) both sides changed differently since base; nothing else decides. It is named for the agent by its entity's identity from the entity index's :identity-schemas ([:var/name s], [:seon.task/id id], [:seon.doc/address a]). Schema properties, read once into the entity index: {:seon.db/root-only true} on the run-record attributes (:seon.agent/boot, started-ms, holder, status, ended-ms), a branch's write of one refused at the write; {:seon.db/derived true} values are never applied and are derived again after the merge for what moved (seon.task/merged-cites-tx generalized); merge entries are branch-local; a moved :schema/key entity re-derives the registry. Everything else merges whole. Deletes (each list's job after: the one rule): stated-keys (+ :held-only :needs :carries :kind-of), stated-attr?, carried-namespace?, data-attr?/data-attr-set/the data-attrs argument, the content predicates, schema-attr?, the merge's use of program/attr-namespaces (moves-program? keeps it, named as its own later item), own-datoms' \"db\" filter (Datahike's system schema instead), judge-tx's task carry (a refine merges into its parent by the same merge), the octopus member selection (each ready branch merges alone), shared?, owned?, overtaken, overtaken-base, deleted-by?, tx-branch ownership, delta-three-way, carried-tx, merge-tx. Estimate, lb76's real change (4,849 added, 168 retracted, 875 source txs): d/changes O(d) ~10M [H, deps]; main's change at those [e a] ~5k seeks x 5k = 25M [H]; naming ~0; apply ~150M checked [H]; ~185M a merge, one per seon_done cached by (base, head) commit ids. Lower bound: O(d) reads and the checked apply of what the branch wrote. Measured today [M, data-2 at root 2026-11-15]: own-datoms 3.1B ticks, apply 98M unchecked, ~3.3B a merge, ~20B a seon_done (6 recomputations). Proofs, red first on an lb76-shaped fixture and read-only on real branches at root before landing (docs/merging.md#a-merge-change-is-proved-on-a-real-branch). After it: seon.db/the-swept-base-branches-are-replayed-onto-main-then-dropped. DEPS REVIEW (Datahike side, 2026-11-15, owner's ask): APPROVED. (Q1) d/changes walks each commit's :base and a merge built on main's value has main as its base, so base..head is the branch's own commits plus merge-main's re-applied own content, netted by [e a v], once each; the other parent's content never appears [read, proof datahike.versioning-test/a-commit-of-a-speculated-value-keeps-its-changes]. (Q2) the source's instant goes under a :seon.tx/* attribute, never as the new tx's :db/txInstant (as-of by date would disagree with as-of by tx); new instants are main's monotone ones: 875 replayed txs take 157 ms and leave main's newest instant 717 ms ahead of the wall clock [M result/dbcdc6a7], strictly increasing, to be stated in docs/merging.md. (Q3) into main: Datahike's merge! through main's writer (datahike.writer/merge-db!, both parents, :datahike/expected-basis-t), which orders it with main's other writes and is the CAS on main; force-branch! (on the one write-commit!) bypasses the writer's order and stays for merge-main into a branch. MEASURED at root on a real branch with a base (tests' pilot, 1,266 datoms, 147 source txs