main}; commit-pending! reads the parents from the batch's reports (merge-parents-of) and writes ONE commit with both; any refusal is abandon-pending! (all or none). merge! alone writes one tx, so it is not the replay's path. To confirm with deps: (a) 'merge! in the write-fn-map passes seon.db's check as 'transact! does; (b) the writer commits any pending batch before a merge's first apply!, so a merge never shares its commit with other writes. FLOW REVIEW: flow's verdict (2026-11-15): (1) the merge computes off the writer, through the existing effects owner (seon.fx, a worker of its own kind), never a new proc: the worker answers tx-data computed at main@t; the writer applies it while main is still at t. If main moved, the writer reads d/changes(main t..now) (~0.08M ticks a commit [M result/c318a0c7]) and asks again only when that touches an [e a] the branch changed; otherwise it applies: the cost follows what moved, and no livelock. runtime's :spawn merge takes the same path. (2) Landing 10: :program-t says only that the program moved (O(1)); the re-prove's input is d/changes from the proof's main t to now, its changed program keys, then tests-reaching, so a move its reach doesn't touch re-proves nothing. SLICE 1 BUILT (jolt/data, 2026-11-15): main into a branch is judged by seon.merge/decide over the branch's facts only (O(what the branch changed)), built as main's head plus the branch's own change (seon.merge/carry, replayed through the check, seon.writer/replay), and committed with its change against the branch head it replaces (seon.merge/merge-record via datahike force-branch! :changes, deps 0fb0383f; exact by net presence, seon.merge-test/a-merge-states-what-main-moved-for-the-branch). MEASURED at root [result/bb17acf5]: a fresh branch ~86 ms; stale tests-meas6 ~4.9 s, 4.46 s of it reading main's change records (main's churn: the churn-writes task next). Rejected on measurement: replaying main's change onto the branch (0.5B to 12B a merge, result/aac1799c). Also in the slice: :seon.db/root-only on what root alone writes, refused at the agent's one write (seon.agent/transact!); seon.turn no longer copies :seon.agent/max-turns, begin-run! no longer writes :seon.agent/task (root's alone, root's ruling); attach! writes the start before the spawn and a refused spawn inverts it exactly; resolve! settles an entity by its symbol or name, bringing a missing ref target from main; one entry shape ([:datum e a]). REVIEWS: context OK (status block, turn; one shape asked and done), runtime APPROVED (agent.clj, server.clj, the rail; a :spawning agent from a dead boot is reaped, reconcile.clj:208/571), flow APPROVED f723ca5fd9 (the exact undo; one transaction taken). Deps reviewed the Datahike side (:changes). At the land: the 54 older entries on branches cleaned once (result/a3ce40e8). WHERE IT RUNS (flow, 2026-11-15, answering data): a :merge kind in the existing effects mechanism, declared in resources/seon/system.edn's :fx with its owner and workers (seon.fx/procs), workload :compute; its perform is the pure decide/carry/replay/record fn over db references {:branch :commit :t}; the writer applies the answer only if main is still at t (apply-if-unmoved, flow's landing-6 review), else the asker asks again. A kind as data, no new mechanism; it also lifts seon.writer/a-merge-is-computed-off-the-writers-step's stall. STATE 2026-11-16 (data): slice 1 lands (main into a branch: decide, carry, replay, merge-record; the root-only rail). Its red, seon.agent-test/a-wake-shows-what-moved-not-the-page-again, is green with merge-record. Proved read-only on real branches at root: session/tests' branch clean, 664 keys all the branch's [M result/b1246b48]; a branch 25,940 t stale refused typed at a task its own write left without :seon.task/execution [M result/bffd83fc]. Measured: decide 120.8M ticks on 1,834 ops (4.8x the 25M estimate; its split owed). NEXT: (a) after the land, retract the old-shape merge entries left on branches (18 :var, 6 :section, 2 :task keys: decide and resolve! read only [:datum e a] keys) through the writer's :tx per branch; (b) restate seon.merge-cost-test for build O(d_branch) + record O(main's records); (c) slice 2: a branch into main by the same rule through datahike.writer's merge path, deleting delta-three-way, carried-tx, tx-branch, owned?, shared?, overtaken-base, deleted-by?, attribute-declarations and judge-tx's three-way."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-branch-merges-whole-into-the-branch-it-came-from seon.merge-test/a-branch-that-deletes-a-var-main-holds-takes-main seon.merge-test/merge-main-on-a-stale-branch-changes-only-its-own-keys seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-costs-the-branchs-change seon.merge-test/a-datum-conflict-is-shown-and-settled seon.merge-test/a-replayed-op-the-check-refuses-refuses-the-merge-whole seon.db-test/a-branch-write-of-a-root-only-attribute-is-refused seon.program-test/a-schema-entry-added-on-a-branch-moves-the-program seon.merge-test/a-derived-value-is-made-again-after-a-merge-never-carried} :seon.task/estimate {seon.merge/merge {:n 5017, :order :linear, :ticks 225000000, :bytes 1000000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-of-main-costs-what-the-branch-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists [M, 2026-11-14, three scratch copies of context's branch, main 1,274 names ahead, 3,526 keys applied]: seon.agent/merge-main! costs 8.22B ticks and 47.5 GB after b5d5336f81 (it was 10.26B and 52.2 GB: merge-tx speculated its whole transaction to read the merged registry, now only the schema entries). What is left, by part: seon.program/load-changes! 2.65B ticks and 30.2 GB, nearly all seon.program/load-form!'s read and eval of 1,028 forms (2.5M ticks, 29 MB a form; contract/arm! and note-loaded! ~0); datahike.api/merge-db 3.47B and 7.5 GB for the one commit; seon.merge/carried-tx 0.83B and 2.8 GB (d/datoms-diff base src over the whole db); delta-three-way 0.71B and 2.5 GB. Estimate from the parts: ~1.2-1.7B ticks and ~3 GB. The design, the larger lever [H]: an agent behind main loads main's changed forms itself though the fork server holds them compiled; a merge of main re-forks the agent from the warm fork server (env = main's head) and loads only its branch's own diff (seon.program/load-diff!, 400-450 ms per its docstring), so a merge costs O(the branch's change), never O(main's moves). Then carried-tx diffs the branch's keys, not the whole db. Deps' share: an eval cache by form digest (Jolt), and merge-db's cost per datom (Datahike), re-measured after the re-fork. Proof, red first: seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-costs-the-branchs-change, a branch two forms ahead of a main that moved 100 forms merges in O(2) form loads, its estimate from parts. Re-measured 2026-11-14 16:15 [M] (scratch spawn at context's branch commit, main 1,484 names ahead, 4,039 keys applied): merge-main! 9.14B ticks, 51.9 GB; datahike.api/merge-db 3.82B / 8.56 GB for 121,919 datoms (~31k ticks and 70 KB a datom, against Datahike's 12.6k and 22 KB a datom by d/with: the per-datom excess is deps', sent); program/load-changes! 2.81B (1,117 load-form!s, 2.46M ticks each); merge-tx 1.09B (carried-tx 0.97B); delta-three-way 0.89B. The design, estimated first (owner, AGENTS §3: estimate algorithmically, 90% for 10%): the merged branch is main's head plus the branch's own diff, made where it is small. (1) The branch's own change since its base, d datoms (its diff from base, shared structure skipped), and main's changed keys since the base (keys only, for conflicts: a key both changed stays unmerged on the branch, as today); (2) the merged db = (d/db-with main-head (branch's own diff, less conflicts)): O(d); (3) one commit: datahike.versioning/force-branch! of that db onto the branch with parents #{branch-head main-commit} and :expected-current-commit the branch head (a compare-and-set; a branch that moved since answers :seon.merge/branch-moved, read and asked again, never a retry); (4) the agent's connection is stale after a force (force-branch!'s docstring), so the agent re-forks from the warm fork server (main's program held) and loads only its branch's forms (seon.program/load-diff!): the one hot path for loading a branch over main. Deletes: merge-main!'s load-changes! of main's moves, merge-tx's apply of main's datoms onto the branch, and carried-tx's whole-db diff. Estimate: d x 31k ticks (~0.1B at d of a few thousand) + the diff of the branch's own nodes + one fork (~65 ms of fork(2)) + k x 2.46M ticks for the branch's k changed forms: ~0.2-0.5B ticks against 9.14B, 20-40x. Proof, red first: seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-costs-the-branchs-change, a branch two forms ahead of a main that moved 100 forms merges in O(2) form loads and O(the branch's datoms) written, held to that arithmetic. Verify first [H]: force-branch! accepts a db-with value (it flushes the speculative db's nodes) and the branch's own diff is O(its change), not O(db). Agreed 2026-11-14 by data-2, runtime, context-4 and deps (root's ask: ONE merge function): seon.merge/merge-main [conn branch base head main]. (1) data-2: conflicts judged over the keys the branch changed only (datoms-diff base head is O(its change) [M: 11.4k datoms, 69M ticks]): by program key for the program (program-of on those keys only) and by [e a] for every other fact, main's content against base's; identity by content stays (a new branch entity whose unique value main holds is clean when equal, a conflict otherwise); any conflict stops it. (2) data-2: clean, the merged db is main's head plus the branch's own datoms, its retractions included (db-with), written as one datahike.versioning/force-branch! with parents #{head main} and :expected-current-commit head (verified on a :memory store; deps keeps force-branch! for this job, distinct from merge-db's own-head absorb). The octopus merge into main is the same function with the sides swapped, never a second. (3) runtime: merge-main! no longer loads main's changed forms into the running agent (a process keeps its program until its next start, docs/plan.md item 3): it marks the program behind its branch, and at the run's next :waiting between turns (after the turn's record, before the next model call; context-4), the agent proc asks root to re-fork it from the warm fork server under its key, its walk value carried in the spawn's fork args (turn number and inbox are branch facts), and the resume path load-diff!s only the branch's own forms; a run past its cap or :done/:failed ends instead. Proofs: the next turn's request bytes equal across the re-fork; a branch two forms ahead of a main that moved 100 merges in O(2) form loads and O(its own datoms) written. Order: after data-2's 5297ca208e; data-2 builds (1)+(2), runtime (3) and the call."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-merge-of-main-costs-the-branchs-change}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-branchs-own-writes-are-read-at-their-cost"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by data-2, 2026-11-15 00:58 [M, root REPL]: counting, over every branch of the standing store, the task and var facts a branch wrote before main's later write there (root's question after seon.merge/overtaken) cost 2.4B ticks and 9.8 GB for a few dozen branches, of which each holds tens to hundreds of its own transactions. The read per branch: seon.db/branch-base (its merge base with main), the branch's transactions by :seon.tx/branch (an :aevt walk of every transaction's branch, every branch's and main's), and per attribute an :aevt walk of the attribute on the head. Lower bound: O(branches x own writes): each branch's own transactions are a seek of :seon.tx/branch at its name (:avet, needs the attribute indexed), and its own datoms come from those transactions (the log, or datoms-diff of base and head, which seon.merge/own-datoms already reads). Cause [H]: two whole-index walks per branch (all transactions' branch, all of an attribute's datoms) where a seek of its own suffices; the 9.8 GB is chiefly the walk of shared history. Estimate: ~40 branches x (one merge-base, ~300 own datoms x a seek ~10k ticks) ~= 150M ticks, ~16x under today. Proof, red first: a cost proof that reading a branch's own writes is constant in main's history and linear in the branch's writes. Owner: data. Proof: the cost proof at two sizes (branches x own writes), constant in main's history; the no-merge-base answer is its own task, seon.merge/a-branch-walk-answers-no-merge-base."
